  "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\u2207f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\u2207f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=\u27e8t,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+{t}^{3}-2\u27e9,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\u27e80,-1\u27e9, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =\u27e8\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\u2207f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}\\cdot ({\\pi }^{3}-3)-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
